################ WHAT IT SAYS

1. CHROMIUM REPRODUCES 21 ELEMENT FOR ELEMENT, 3/3 runs. Not just the total — the arithmetic
   `filterBudget.ts` prints:

     settled nine   g 2 · toggle-sun 1 · toggle-moon 1 · boil-pose 3 + is-active 1 · sparkle 1  =  9
     rest poses     svg.rest-pose 6 + svg.rest-pose.is-pose-active 2                            = +8
     wordmark       g.logo-pose 3 + g.logo-pose.is-active 1                                     = +4
                                                                                          total  21

   Every row of the pass-4 device breakdown re-derives on an independent instrument. The
   figure was n=1 and it is no longer alone.

2. BOTH RULES AGREE AT THE PEAK, which is row 3's whole claim. spec == device in all six runs
   (21/21 chromium, 20/20 webkit): before the bake lands nothing is `display: none` yet, so
   consulting own-display changes nothing. The claim held on a second instrument.

3. THE SETTLED FLOOR RE-DERIVES TOO: 9 / 13 in every run, both engines — rows 1 and 2 of the
   same table, unchanged.

4. WEBKIT PEAKS AT 20, AND THE MISSING ONE HAS A NAME: `svg.sparkle-icon`. Every other row is
   identical in name and count. It is a sampling-window artifact, not an absent surface —
   WebKit's peak lands at t≈96-101ms against Chromium's t≈291-472ms, i.e. before the sparkle
   joins the population, and WebKit's own SETTLED count is 9, which includes it. The boot
   window is simply shorter on WebKit. No surface is missing; nothing is over budget.

5. THE TRIGGER DOES NOT FIRE. It reads "a second cold-load reading ABOVE 21". Max across six
   cold contexts, two engines: 21. Nothing above. The 21 stays UNGATED with its trigger intact
   — and now with a corroborating reading under it rather than a single session.

